    "is_reverted": false,
    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: The current DelegationMutex logic is directionally correct, but the implementation is too broad and does not guarantee that mutex roles are only held simultaneously during Emergency if at least one is held via delegation. The failed assertion and instance analysis show the fix must be tightened as per the detailed counterexample analysis.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Satisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency fails)\n- Instances: Meaningful (but missing required Emergency/mutex/delegation scenario)\n- Vacuity: None\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\nRefine the DelegationMutex fact to enforce the following:\n\n1. In Emergency mode, a user may only hold both of a mutually exclusive role pair if at least one is held via delegation (never both by direct assignment). The current fact permits too much by allowing the case where a user could theoretically hold both via direct assignment if any delegation exists, which is not intended.\n\n2. Update the relevant portion of the DelegationMutex fact as follows (replace the Emergency-mode block only):\n\nReplace:\n```\nall s: State |\n    s.mode = Emergency =>\n        all u: User |\n            all r1, r2: Role |\n                r2 in r1.mutexWith =>\n                    not (r1 in s.userRoles[u] and r2 in s.userRoles[u])\n                    or (some d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations and (d.role = r1 or d.role = r2))\n```\nWith stricter logic:\n```\nall s: State |\n    s.mode = Emergency =>\n        all u: User |\n            all r1, r2: Role |\n                r2 in r1.mutexWith =>\n                    // User may hold both mutex roles iff (at least one is via delegation), and never both via direct assignment\n                    (r1 in s.userRoles[u] and r2 in s.userRoles[u]) =>\n                        ( (some d1: Delegation | d1.to = u and d1.role = r1 and d1 in s.activeDelegations)\n                          or\n                          (some d2: Delegation | d2.to = u and d2.role = r2 and d2 in s.activeDelegations)\n                        )\n                        and not (r1 in directRoles[s, u] and r2 in directRoles[s, u])\n```\nRationale: This ensures that, in Emergency, a user can only hold both mutex roles if at least one is via delegation, and never both directly—matching the requirement and enabling the assertion to pass.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - requirements are clear\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): Refine DelegationMutex fact as per above to enforce that in Emergency, mutex role pairs may only be held together if at least one is delegated and never both via direct assignment.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): After making the fix, rerun assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency and the positive scenario predicate MutexPossibleDuringEmergency to ensure the required Emergency mutex-with-delegation scenario is now possible.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): Consider adding explicit negative assertions to confirm that both mutex roles are never held directly, even in Emergency, for defense in depth.\n```",
